      case _ => return acc\n  acc\n```\n\nThis is cleaner \u2014 match on `current` (the operator), then consume it, then get the RHS.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "317954c7-197a-4bec-8712-dc294184cc73", "tool_call": {"id": "317954c7-197a-4bec-8712-dc294184cc73", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The issue is that `consume()` returns `Token` (the sealed trait), not `Op`. So `op.ch` doesn't work because `Token` doesn't have a `ch` member.\\n\\nI have two options:\\n1. Change `consume()` to return `Op` \\u2014 but that's not type-safe since `consume` can return `Num` or `Eof` too.\\n2. Use pattern matching on `op` instead of `op.ch`.\\n\\nOption 2 is cleaner. Instead of:\\n```scala\\nval op = consume()\\nop.ch match { case '+' => ... }\\n```\\n\\nI'll use:\\n```scala\\nconsume() match {\\n  case Op('+') => acc = acc + term()\\n  case Op('-') => acc = acc - term()\\n}\\n```\\n\\nBut wait, I need to be careful about the structure. Let me restructure the expression/term methods to use pattern matching on the consumed token directly.\\n\\nActually, a simpler approach: I can change the consume method to return the token, then pattern match on it. Or I can just match on `current` before consuming.\\n\\nLet me restructure:\\n```scala\\ndef expression(): Int =\\n  var acc = term()\\n  while hasMore && (current == Op('+') || current == Op('-')) do\\n    consume() // consume the operator\\n    current match\\n      case Op('+') => acc = acc + term()\\n      case Op('-') => acc = acc - term()\\n  acc\\n```\\n\\nWait, but after `consume()`, `current` will be the next token. If the next token is `+` or `-`, that's wrong \\u2014 it should be a number. Let me think again...\\n\\nActually the issue is: after `consume()`, the current token is the RHS number (Num), not the operator. So I need to match on the consumed token, not on current.\\n\\nBetter approach:\\n```scala\\ndef expression(): Int =\\n  var acc 